# Derivation of the Cartan Structure Equations

## 1. Setup and definitions

Let $e^a{}_mu$ be the vielbein (tetrad) and $\omega^a{}_{\mu b}$ the spin connection. Greek indices $\mu,\nu,\rho$ are coordinate (world) indices; Latin indices $a,b,c$ are Lorentz (tangent-space) indices. The metric is $g_{\mu\nu} = \eta_{ab}\,e^a{}_mu\,e^b{}_nu$, with $\eta_{ab}$ the flat Minkowski metric.

The **covariant derivative** acting on a Lorentz vector $V^a$ is
$$D_\mu V^a = \partial_\mu V^a + \omega^a{}_{\mu b}\,V^b.$$

The **torsion tensor** and **curvature tensor** are defined by the failure of covariant derivatives to commute, or equivalently by the action of the commutator on a vector:
$$[D_\mu, D_\nu] V^a = R^a{}_{b\mu\nu}\,V^b - T^\rho{}_{\mu\nu}\,D_\rho V^a.$$

Equivalently, the two field strengths are defined directly:
$$T^a{}_{\mu\nu} := \partial_\mu e^a{}_nu - \partial_\nu e^a{}_mu + \omega^a{}_{\mu b}\,e^b{}_nu - \omega^a{}_{\nu b}\,e^b{}_mu,$$
$$R^a{}_{b\mu\nu} := \partial_\mu \omega^a{}_{\nu b} - \partial_\nu \omega^a{}_{\mu b} + \omega^a{}_{\mu c}\,\omega^c{}_{\nu b} - \omega^a{}_{\nu c}\,\omega^c{}_{\mu b}.$$

---

## 2. Dual form notation

Introduce the vielbein 1-form and spin-connection 1-form:
$$e^a = e^a{}_mu\,dx^\mu, \qquad \omega^a{}_b = \omega^a{}_{\mu b}\,dx^\mu.$$

The exterior derivative of a $p$-form $\alpha$ is $d\alpha$, and for a 1-form $\alpha = \alpha_\mu dx^\mu$,
$$d\alpha = \partial_\nu \alpha_\mu\,dx^\nu \wedge dx^\mu = \tfrac12(\partial_\nu\alpha_\mu - \partial_\mu\alpha_\nu)\,dx^\nu\wedge dx^\mu.$$

The **gauge-covariant exterior derivative** $D$ acts on a Lorentz-valued $p$-form $\Phi^a$ as
$$D\Phi^a = d\Phi^a + \omega^a{}_b \wedge \Phi^b.$$

---

## 3. First Cartan structure equation (torsion)

Compute $De^a$:
$$De^a = de^a + \omega^a{}_b \wedge e^b.$$

Expand the exterior derivative of $e^a = e^a{}_mu\,dx^\mu$:
$$de^a = \partial_\nu e^a{}_mu\,dx^\nu \wedge dx^\mu = \tfrac12\bigl(\partial_\nu e^a{}_mu - \partial_\mu e^a{}_nu\bigr)\,dx^\nu \wedge dx^\mu.$$

Expand the wedge term:
$$\omega^a{}_b \wedge e^b = \omega^a{}_{\nu b}\,e^b{}_mu\,dx^\nu \wedge dx^\mu = \tfrac12\bigl(\omega^a{}_{\nu b}\,e^b{}_mu - \omega^a{}_{\mu b}\,e^b{}_nu\bigr)\,dx^\nu \wedge dx^\mu.$$

Adding and reading off the coefficient of $\tfrac12\,dx^\nu\wedge dx^\mu$:
$$\tfrac12\bigl(\partial_\nu e^a{}_mu - \partial_\mu e^a{}_nu + \omega^a{}_{\nu b}\,e^b{}_mu - \omega^a{}_{\mu b}\,e^b{}_nu\bigr).$$

Relabel $\nu\leftrightarrow\mu$ in the sign to compare with the definition of $T^a{}_{\mu\nu}$:
$$T^a{}_{\mu\nu} = \partial_\mu e^a{}_nu - \partial_\nu e^a{}_mu + \omega^a{}_{\mu b}\,e^b{}_nu - \omega^a{}_{\nu b}\,e^b{}_mu.$$

This is exactly (up to the overall sign convention from $dx^\nu\wedge dx^\mu = -dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu$) the coefficient we obtained. Therefore

$$\boxed{T^a = De^a = de^a + \omega^a{}_b \wedge e^b.}$$

This is the **first Cartan structure equation**. In components, $T^a = \tfrac12\,T^a{}_{\mu\nu}\,dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu$, recovering the defining expression for $T^a{}_{\mu\nu}$.

### Torsion-free case

Imposing $T^a = 0$ gives
$$de^a + \omega^a{}_b \wedge e^b = 0.$$

This algebraic equation determines $\omega^a{}_b$ in terms of $e^a$ (the **torsion-free spin connection**).

---

## 4. Second Cartan structure equation (curvature)

Compute $D\omega^a{}_b$. By definition of the covariant exterior derivative on a Lorentz-valued 1-form in the adjoint (index $b$ is also acted on):
$$D\omega^a{}_b = d\omega^a{}_b + \omega^a{}_c \wedge \omega^c{}_b.$$

Expand $d\omega^a{}_b = \partial_\mu\omega^a{}_{\nu b}\,dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu = \tfrac12(\partial_\mu\omega^a{}_{\nu b} - \partial_\nu\omega^a{}_{\mu b})\,dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu$.

Expand the wedge:
$$\omega^a{}_c \wedge \omega^c{}_b = \omega^a{}_{\mu c}\,\omega^c{}_{\nu b}\,dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu = \tfrac12\bigl(\omega^a{}_{\mu c}\,\omega^c{}_{\nu b} - \omega^a{}_{\nu c}\,\omega^c{}_{\mu b}\bigr)\,dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu.$$

Adding, the coefficient of $\tfrac12\,dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu$ is
$$\partial_\mu\omega^a{}_{\nu b} - \partial_\nu\omega^a{}_{\mu b} + \omega^a{}_{\mu c}\,\omega^c{}_{\nu b} - \omega^a{}_{\nu c}\,\omega^c{}_{\mu b},$$

which is precisely $R^a{}_{b\mu\nu}$ as defined. Hence

$$\boxed{R^a{}_b = D\omega^a{}_b = d\omega^a{}_b + \omega^a{}_c \wedge \omega^c{}_b.}$$

This is the **second Cartan structure equation**. In components, $R^a{}_b = \tfrac12\,R^a{}_{b\mu\nu}\,dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu$.

---

## 5. Metric compatibility and antisymmetry of the spin connection

Metric compatibility requires $D_\mu \eta_{ab} = 0$, i.e. the covariant derivative preserves the Minkowski metric. Since $\partial_\mu \eta_{ab} = 0$ (constant), this demands
$$\omega_{\mu ab} + \omega_{\mu ba} = 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \omega_{\mu ab} = -\omega_{\mu ba}.$$

Lowering the Lorentz index with $\eta_{ab}$: $\omega_{\mu ab} = \eta_{ac}\,\omega^c{}_{\mu b}$. The antisymmetry $\omega_{\mu ab} = -\omega_{\mu ba}$ means the spin connection takes values in the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{so}(1,3)$ (the Lorentz algebra). This is the **antisymmetry condition** stated in the problem.

Consequences:
- $R_{ab\mu\nu} = -R_{ba\mu\nu}$ (curvature is antisymmetric in its Lorentz indices), since $R^a{}_b$ inherits the Lie-algebra-valued structure: $R_{ab} = -R_{ba}$.
- The curvature 2-form $R^a{}_b$ is also $\mathfrak{so}(1,3)$-valued.

---

## 6. Consistency check via the commutator

To confirm the structure equations reproduce the defining commutator, act with $[D_\mu,D_\nu]$ on a vector $V^a$:
$$[D_\mu,D_\nu]V^a = \bigl(\partial_\mu\omega^a{}_{\nu b} - \partial_\nu\omega^a{}_{\mu b} + \omega^a{}_{\mu c}\omega^c{}_{\nu b} - \omega^a{}_{\nu c}\omega^c{}_{\mu b}\bigr)V^b = R^a{}_{b\mu\nu}\,V^b.$$

Acting on the vielbein $e^a{}_rho$ (which carries both a Lorentz index $a$ and a world index $\rho$), the commutator also picks up the Christoffel part from the world index; the difference between the full Levi-Civita commutator and the pure Lorentz part is the torsion term:
$$[D_\mu,D_\nu]e^a{}_rho = R^a{}_{b\mu\nu}\,e^b{}_rho - T^\sigma{}_{\mu\nu}\,D_\sigma e^a{}_rho.$$

Equivalently, applying $D$ to the first structure equation $T^a = De^a$ and using the **Ricci identity** $D^2 e^a = R^a{}_b \wedge e^b$:
$$DT^a = D(De^a) = D^2 e^a = R^a{}_b \wedge e^b.$$

This is the **first Bianchi identity**:
$$\boxed{DT^a = R^a{}_b \wedge e^b.}$$

In the torsion-free case ($T^a = 0$) this reduces to $R^a{}_b \wedge e^b = 0$, the **first Bianchi identity for Riemannian geometry** (cyclic identity $R^a{}_{[b\mu\nu]}=0$ after converting indices).

Applying $D$ to the second structure equation gives the **second Bianchi identity**:
$$DR^a{}_b = D(D\omega^a{}_b) = dR^a{}_b + \omega^a{}_c\wedge R^c{}_b - \omega^c{}_b\wedge R^a{}_c = 0,$$
i.e.
$$\boxed{DR^a{}_b = 0.}$$

This follows because $D^2\omega^a{}_b = [R^a{}_c,\,\cdot\,]$ applied to the connection itself closes (the Bianchi identity $DD\omega = R\wedge\omega - \omega\wedge R + dR = 0$ is the Jacobi identity of the curvature).

---

## 7. Summary

From the definitions of $T^a{}_{\mu\nu}$ and $R^a{}_{b\mu\nu}$ in terms of $e^a{}_mu$ and $\omega^a{}_{\mu b}$, and using the gauge-covariant exterior derivative $D = d + \omega\wedge$, we derived:

| Equation | Form | Components |
|---|---|---|
| **1st Cartan (torsion)** | $T^a = De^a = de^a + \omega^a{}_b\wedge e^b$ | $T^a{}_{\mu\nu} = \partial_\mu e^a{}_nu - \partial_\nu e^a{}_mu + \omega^a{}_{\mu b}e^b{}_nu - \omega^a{}_{\nu b}e^b{}_mu$ |
| **2nd Cartan (curvature)** | $R^a{}_b = D\omega^a{}_b = d\omega^a{}_b + \omega^a{}_c\wedge\omega^c{}_b$ | $R^a{}_{b\mu\nu} = \partial_\mu\omega^a{}_{\nu b} - \partial_\nu\omega^a{}_{\mu b} + \omega^a{}_{\mu c}\omega^c{}_{\nu b} - \omega^a{}_{\nu c}\omega^c{}_{\mu b}$ |
| **1st Bianchi** | $DT^a = R^a{}_b\wedge e^b$ | — |
| **2nd Bianchi** | $DR^a{}_b = 0$ | — |

Under the **torsion-free** condition $T^a = 0$, the first equation becomes $de^a + \omega^a{}_b\wedge e^b = 0$, which algebraically determines $\omega^a{}_b$ from $e^a$. Under **metric compatibility** ($D_\mu\eta_{ab}=0$), the spin connection is antisymmetric: $\omega_{\mu ab} = -\omega_{\mu ba}$, so $\omega^a{}_b \in \mathfrak{so}(1,3)$, and the curvature inherits $R_{ab\mu\nu} = -R_{ba\mu\nu}$.

The two structure equations are the field-strength decompositions of the gauge-covariant exterior derivative: torsion is the covariant curl of the vielbein, curvature is the covariant curl of the spin connection — exactly the vielbein/spin-connection analogues of $F = dA + A\wedge A$ in Yang–Mills theory, with the Lorentz group as the gauge group.

